  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe stacked upper level low is slowly moving to the east today \nwith ridging starting to build back in. Weak warm air advection \nwith the ridge building in will help high temperatures climb \ninto the upper 70s and low 80s Tuesday into Wednesday. Surface \nhigh pressure and dry air in place will keep the region dry and \nmostly sunny the next couple of days before some high clouds \nbegin to advect in Wednesday. With the surface high in place \nalong with clear skies, valley fog formation is expected once \nagain tonight. Increasing surface winds and drier air in place \ntomorrow may lower the coverage of morning fog Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nAn area of low pressure that forms in the lee of the northern \nRockies today will pass north of the Great Lakes Wednesday night\ninto Thursday dragging a cold front through the region. Models \nand ensembles have come into better consistency with the front \npassing through late Wednesday night into early Thursday \nmorning. Ensemble probabilities of Most Unstable CAPE >500 and \nshear >30 knots has fallen down to around 10%. Picking out the \nensemble members that meet the criteria, have the front passing \nthrough later Thursday and given they are outliers, confidence \nis high that the severe thunderstorms are unlikely. Still given \nsome elevated CAPE in model soundings, a few thunderstorms \nembedded in the stratiform precipitation along the front is \nlikely. \n\nRainfall with the front is a higher concern but will likely \ncause little to no problems. Precipitable Water values are still\nup between 1.5 and 2 inches with decent moisture being advected\npoleward ahead of the front. Deterministic models have backed \noff on a secondary low forming along the front so the potential \nfor a more prolonged period of rain is waning. The front looks \nto be fairly progressive as it moves through so training \nthunderstorm threat is also lowering. Brief heavy rainfall \nrates of 1 to 2 inches per hour in some of the thunderstorms may\nlead to localized urban flooding if they occur over a town or \ncity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nOnce the front is through, we enter a period of westerly Zonal \nflow with not many shortwaves in the flow, at least not modeled \ncurrently right now. With surface high pressure building back \nin, conditions Friday into next weekend may be very similar to \nthis stretch of weather that we just had this weekend. 850 mb \ntemperatures are a few degrees cooler so temperatures may be in \nthe upper 60s and low 70s rather than low to mid 70s like they \nwere this weekend.",
